#!/usr/bin/env bash
# reflex-cadence: 9-59/15 * * * *
# reflex-args: --edge
# mesh-perimeter — the mesh's ONE situational-awareness read of its surroundings: OUTSIDE + NETWORK +
# PHYSICAL fused into a single CALM/NOTICE/ALERT verdict. No single sense answers "is anything unusual
# around?" — together they do:
#   OUTSIDE  (mesh-watchtower)     — the public-IP vantage: external scanners/knocks, reach, exposure.
#   NETWORK  (mesh-lan-newdevice   — a NEW/unknown device on the home LAN (router DHCP delta), PLUS
#            +mesh-gateway-identity) the gateway IP→MAC binding canary: a flipped gateway MAC is the
#                                  ARP-spoof/MITM/router-swap signal a device census can't see —
#                                  raises NETWORK to ALERT (fresh flip) / NOTICE (flip within 30m,
#                                  already auto-rebaselined by the canary). READ-ONLY on the canary's
#                                  cron log (no double-writer on its change-gated baseline).
#   PHYSICAL (mesh-presence        — unknown BLE devices in radio range (rough proximity), PLUS
#            +mesh-motion-attribution) device-free MOTION × operator-home: motion while the operator is
#                                  AWAY with no carried device (UNEXPLAINED) = the device-less intruder
#                                  BLE alone misses → raises PHYSICAL to NOTICE (honest-fusion gated),
#            +mesh-stranger-watch   visual identity: neither BLE nor motion can say WHO — a fresh,
#                                  2-of-2-confirmed camera stranger read (mesh-face-recognize, via
#                                  stranger-watch's own state, READ-ONLY, no extra vision call) raises
#                                  PHYSICAL straight to ALERT (honest-fusion gated),
#            +mesh-powerbtn)        chassis touch: the ACPI power-button IRQ counter fires only on a
#                                  REAL press — the one thing the room senses can't feel. A recent
#                                  press (read from powerbtn.log, the */5 --edge cron sink, READ-ONLY
#                                  — never runs the tool, whose delta counter is single-consumer)
#                                  while mesh-operator-home says AWAY (fresh) raises PHYSICAL to
#                                  NOTICE; HOME/UNCERTAIN/stale never escalate (honest-fusion gated).
#            +mesh-soundscape)      the ROOM EAR: the only physical channel that works in the dark,
#                                  around a corner, with no device on the intruder. READ-ONLY on the
#                                  ear's */10 log + liveness tick — a window the GRINDER rejected as
#                                  `(transient …b/s — a bang, not a rhythm)`/`(beatless …b)` at a
#                                  real score IS the perimeter's signal (same measure tract, opposite
#                                  sign). Impulse + fresh AWAY → PHYSICAL NOTICE; a stale/absent ear
#                                  reads UNKNOWN and its silence is never taken for quiet.
# It is to the perimeter what mesh-body-context is to the body and mesh-sensorium is to the node:
# a fusion, not a new sensor. Read-only (observes; never acts).
#
# Honest-organ doctrine (memory fusion-tools-distinguish-empty-from-failed): an UNREACHABLE input is
# rendered visibly distinct from a genuine all-clear — a down sensor is never silently a "CALM". The
# overall verdict is the worst REACHABLE axis; if every axis is unreachable it says CANNOT-ASSESS, not
# CALM. NETWORK reads the lan-newdevice baseline READ-ONLY (no mutation) so it never races that reflex.
#
# LITERATURE (live review, 2026-06-29 — predictive processing / Bayesian brain, angle = concrete metric):
# Shafiei, Jesawada, Friston & Russo, "Distributionally robust free energy principle for decision-making"
# (Nature Communications 17:707, 2026; doi:10.1038/s41467-025-67348-6) introduce DR-FREE: choose policies by
# minimizing the MAXIMUM free energy over an ambiguity set, not the expected free energy under one learned
# environment. The measurable mechanism is a minimax ambiguity cost: evaluate plausible environments in
# the ambiguity set and optimize against the worst one. Their experiments use stochastic navigation/control
# benchmarks where ordinary free-energy agents fail under shifted/ambiguous dynamics but robust agents
# complete the task. This is NOT already embodied by honest-fusion. Honest-fusion says "unreachable !=
# clear" in the text; DR-FREE says an action policy can be chosen under the WORST plausible unread axis.
# Concrete application HERE: `PERIMETER_ROBUST_PARTIAL=1` makes a partial all-clear non-CALM. If every
# reachable axis is CALM but at least one axis is unreachable, the worst plausible hidden environment is
# not trusted as clear, so the perimeter emits NOTICE (partial) instead of CALM (partial). Default stays
# unchanged because this can raise operator-facing sensitivity; enable it only for robust posture policy.
#
# ── MARKOV-BLANKET REIFICATION: this perimeter is a Pearl blanket (a map-tool), never a Friston blanket ──
# LITERATURE (live review, 2026-06-22 — predictive processing / the Bayesian brain, angle = a known
# CRITIQUE/failure mode): the un-embodied concept is the PEARL-vs-FRISTON BLANKET conflation, the central
# critique of the free-energy programme's use of the boundary construct. A Markov blanket is the
# statistical boundary (sensory + active states) that conditionally separates a "system" from everything
# else. Bruineberg, Dołęga, Dewhurst & Baltieri, "The Emperor's New Markov Blankets" (Behavioral & Brain
# Sciences 45:e183, 2022; philsci-archive 18467) show the literature slides between two incompatible
# readings: a PEARL blanket — the original instrumental/epistemic device (a partition the MODELLER draws
# to factor a joint distribution; part of the MAP) — and a FRISTON blanket — the metaphysical claim that
# this partition is a REAL boundary in the world, demarcating where an agent/self ends (part of the
# TERRITORY). The reification error is transposing Pearl's empirical credentials onto Friston's
# ontological claim: "the math is not the territory" (Andrews 2021). Live, continuously published debate:
# BBS commentaries "Markov blankets do not demarcate the boundaries of the mind" + "Markov blankets and
# the preformationist assumption" (2022); dynamic-blanket-detection work continuing to 2025.
# WHERE IT BITES — HERE. This tool draws exactly such a boundary: it fuses OUTSIDE/NETWORK/PHYSICAL and
# classifies devices as KNOWN (ours) vs unknown/FOREIGN, then renders one CALM/NOTICE/ALERT verdict — the
# textbook shape of a mesh "self" with a sensory surface and an inside to defend. That partition is a
# PEARL blanket: an instrumental factoring of the sensor stream, drawn for the OPERATOR's situational
# awareness. The reification hazard is reading it as a FRISTON blanket — a real self/non-self skin of a
# "mesh organism" — so that a device crossing the perimeter becomes an ontological INTRUDER across the
# mesh's body rather than a change in the modeller's catalog. This is the formal grounding of the standing
# discipline [[all-is-text-prediction-discipline]] ("no self/non-self tag … own actions are just more text
# to predict"): the perimeter is the one organ most TEMPTED to reify, because tagging inside/outside is
# its whole job.
# DISTINCT FROM the honest-fusion doctrine above (:13-16): that governs REACHABLE-vs-unreachable (a down
# axis is never a silent CALM) — an epistemic-coverage rule. This is a different axis, SELF-vs-world: is
# the inside/outside split a map-tool or a territory-boundary? Honest-fusion keeps the verdict honest
# about what it COULD read; this keeps it honest about what the boundary IS. Also NOT requisite-variety /
# the good-regulator theorem (mesh-homeostasis) and NOT precision-weighting (mesh-precision) — other PP
# threads already embodied; and NOT the FEP dark-room drive (mesh-needs).
# HELD (behavioral, steward/operator): nothing to change in the fuse — it already only observes + reports.
# The inoculation is to keep the partition explicitly INSTRUMENTAL in any future framing: never let
# "perimeter = the mesh's skin / immune boundary" calcify into code that treats KNOWN/FOREIGN as an
# ontological self, and keep CALM/NOTICE/ALERT a situational read for the operator, not a threat-to-the-
# self verdict.
#
#   mesh-perimeter           one fused situational read: 3 axes + overall verdict
#   mesh-perimeter --edge     only emit when verdict changes vs last run (for cron wiring)
#   mesh-perimeter --raw      the raw per-axis facts behind the verdict (the artifact)
#   mesh-perimeter --multiverse  SPECIFICATION-CURVE audit of this verdict's own recipe: replay
#                             ~/.mesh/perimeter.log across every defensible panel x scoring
#                             rule x partial-coverage config, and attribute the disagreement.
#                             read-only, no probe.
#   mesh-perimeter --test     deps + at-least-one-axis reachable gate (exit 2 = nothing to read)
#
# Exit: 0 = a read was produced (verdict is in the TEXT, not rc — sense convention); 2 = could not
# assess (every axis unreachable). LAN intrusion / scan spikes surface as ALERT/NOTICE in the text.
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
[ -n "${HOME:-}" ] || HOME=$(getent passwd "$(id -u)" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f6); export HOME
[ -f "$HOME/.mesh/nodes" ] && . "$HOME/.mesh/nodes"

KNOWN_LAN="$HOME/.mesh/.lan-known-macs"   # baseline OWNED by mesh-lan-newdevice; we only READ it
BLE_WIN="${PERIMETER_BLE_WIN:-8}"
BENIGN_SUPPRESS="${PERIMETER_BENIGN_SUPPRESS:-3}" # suppress known-benign scan spikes after N repeats
BENIGN_STATE="$HOME/.mesh/.perimeter-benign"      # track known-benign fingerprint counts
ROBUST_PARTIAL="${PERIMETER_ROBUST_PARTIAL:-0}"   # opt-in DR-FREE-style worst-case ambiguity guard

# A locally-administered MAC (bit 0x02 of first octet) is privacy-randomized (iOS/Android rotation) —
# an unknown one is far more likely a known device's new random address than an intrusion, so it is a
# NOTICE not an ALERT. A globally-unique (OUI) unknown MAC keeps the stronger ALERT. (Same insight
# mesh-lan-newdevice and mesh-novelty use.)
is_laa() { local o="${1%%:*}"; [ -n "$o" ] && (( (16#$o & 2) != 0 )) 2>/dev/null; }

# ---- FUSE: overall verdict = worst REACHABLE axis (UNREACHABLE contributes coverage, not severity).
# Pure (no I/O) so --test can dry-run the honest-fusion contract — the tool's reason to exist.
rank() { case "$1" in ALERT) echo 3;; NOTICE) echo 2;; CALM) echo 1;; *) echo 0;; esac; }
# fuse_posture <out_sev> <net_sev> <phy_sev> → sets globals `verdict` and `ec` (identical to the old
# inline block). Worst reachable axis wins; an unreachable axis (rank 0) adds coverage not severity;
# all-3-unreachable = CANNOT-ASSESS exit 2 (never a silent CALM); a partial mix is annotated.
fuse_posture() {
  local s r v worst=0 reachable=0 unreach=0
  for s in "$1" "$2" "$3"; do
    r=$(rank "$s")
    if [ "$r" -gt 0 ]; then reachable=$((reachable+1)); [ "$r" -gt "$worst" ] && worst=$r; else unreach=$((unreach+1)); fi
  done
  if [ "$reachable" -eq 0 ]; then verdict="CANNOT-ASSESS (all 3 axes unreachable)"; ec=2
  else
    case "$worst" in 3) v=ALERT;; 2) v=NOTICE;; *) v=CALM;; esac
    if [ "$ROBUST_PARTIAL" = 1 ] && [ "$v" = CALM ] && [ "$unreach" -gt 0 ]; then
      v=NOTICE
    fi
    verdict="$v"; [ "$unreach" -gt 0 ] && verdict="$v (partial — $unreach/3 axes unreachable)"; ec=0
  fi
}
# edge_line <iso-ts> <mark> <host> <out> <net> <phy> <reason> → the ONE line --edge appends to
# ~/.mesh/perimeter.log. Pure (no I/O), so --test drives the REAL emitter instead of grepping for a
# format string. It exists because the shape is load-bearing and was wrong for 572 consecutive lines
# (task:perimeter-log-has-no-timestamps-on-553-lines): the cron wiring is a bare `>>` append of
# stdout, so a field this printf omits is omitted forever and mtime dates only the newest line.
# The ts goes FIRST and is followed by the bracket, because the single consumer
# (mesh-sensor-tape extract_token) strips `^<iso-ts>\s+\S+\s+` before it reads the state token —
# ts-then-bracket is eaten cleanly, bracket-then-ts is not.
edge_line() {
  printf '%s [%s] %s: O=%s N=%s P=%s — %s (mesh-perimeter for full)\n' "$1" "$2" "$3" "$4" "$5" "$6" "$7"
}
# motion_phy_sev <ma_verdict> <ma_rc> <cur_phy_sev> → echoes the (possibly-raised) PHYSICAL severity.
# Folds mesh-motion-attribution (device-free MOTION × operator-home) into the PHYSICAL axis, which BLE
# alone can't answer: motion while the operator is AWAY with no carried device is the real intrusion
# signal. Pure (no I/O) so --test dry-runs it. Honest-fusion contract:
#   - ONLY a confident UNEXPLAINED (rc 0) raises severity, and only to NOTICE — wifi-motion is
#     RSSI-drift radar (noisier than a MAC); flag-to-investigate, never cry wolf at ALERT on one blip.
#   - A degraded/UNKNOWN motion read (rc≠0) NEVER escalates (a dead radar must not manufacture an
#     intruder), and OCCUPANT/ATTRIBUTED/UNATTRIBUTED/QUIET are benign/neutral — no escalation.
#   - Never LOWERS an existing ALERT (e.g. an unknown-global-MAC network-adjacent physical reading).
#   - UNREACHABLE→NOTICE is intended: a reachable confident UNEXPLAINED makes a BLE-blind axis assessable.
motion_phy_sev() {
  local v="$1" rc="$2" cur="$3"
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$v" = "UNEXPLAINED" ] && [ "$cur" != "ALERT" ]; then echo "NOTICE"; else echo "$cur"; fi
}

# gw_net_sev <gw_label> <fresh:0|1> <recent_changed:0|1> <cur_net_sev> → echoes the (possibly-raised)
# NETWORK severity. Folds mesh-gateway-identity (gateway IP→MAC binding canary) into the NETWORK axis,
# which lan-newdevice alone can't answer: the census sees who JOINED the LAN, not whether the gateway
# ITSELF was swapped/spoofed. Pure (no I/O) so --test dry-runs it. Honest-fusion contract:
#   - Only a FRESH canary reading escalates. Newest-line CHANGED (flip caught on the last canary run)
#     → ALERT — a flipped gateway MAC is the canonical ARP-spoof/MITM signature (at least as strong as
#     lan-newdevice's unknown-global-MAC ALERT). Fires on a legit router swap too — worth ALERT either way.
#   - The canary AUTO-REBASELINES, so a flip earlier in the window survives only in its log: a CHANGED
#     within the recent window (newest already STABLE again) → NOTICE — investigate, don't cry wolf twice.
#   - A stale/missing canary log NEVER escalates and never fakes assessability (cur passes through —
#     a dead canary must not manufacture a MITM, nor silently bless the gateway).
#   - NEW (first observation / gateway IP moved networks) is neutral — never escalated.
#   - Never LOWERS an existing ALERT (e.g. an unknown-global-MAC on the LAN).
#   - UNREACHABLE→ALERT/NOTICE is intended: a fresh canary hit makes a DHCP-blind axis assessable.
gw_net_sev() {
  local label="$1" fresh="$2" recent="$3" cur="$4"
  if [ "$fresh" = 1 ] && [ "$label" = CHANGED ]; then echo "ALERT"; return; fi
  if [ "$recent" = 1 ] && [ "$cur" != ALERT ]; then echo "NOTICE"; return; fi
  echo "$cur"
}

# gw_log_scan <canary-log> <now_epoch> [fresh_s=1200] [recent_s=1800] → sets globals gw_label / gw_mac /
# gw_fresh (newest per-run line within fresh_s = 2× the canary's */10 stride) / gw_recent (any CHANGED
# line within recent_s). File-bound + epoch-parameterized so --test drives it on fixtures. Only
# '[gateway-identity] ' emit lines are parsed (the canary's exit-2 UNREACHABLE stderr lines land in the
# same cron log and must not be misread as verdicts). ts = the line's last field (the canary's emit format).
gw_log_scan() {
  local f="$1" now="$2" fresh_s="${3:-1200}" recent_s="${4:-1800}" line ts ep
  gw_label=""; gw_mac=""; gw_fresh=0; gw_recent=0
  [ -r "$f" ] || return 0
  line="$(grep -E '^\[gateway-identity\] ' "$f" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
  [ -n "$line" ] || return 0
  gw_label="$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $2}')"
  gw_mac="$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oE 'mac=[^ ]+' | head -1 | cut -d= -f2)"
  ts="$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $NF}')"
  ep="$(date -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)" || ep=""
  [ -n "$ep" ] && [ $(( now - ep )) -le "$fresh_s" ] && gw_fresh=1
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    ts="$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $NF}')"
    ep="$(date -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)" || continue
    [ -n "$ep" ] && [ $(( now - ep )) -le "$recent_s" ] && gw_recent=1
  done < <(grep -E '^\[gateway-identity\] CHANGED ' "$f" 2>/dev/null | tail -5)
  return 0
}

# stranger_phy_sev <sw_label> <sw_fresh> <cur_phy_sev> → echoes the (possibly-raised) PHYSICAL
# severity. Folds mesh-stranger-watch (camera+Groq-vision person classification, itself already
# 2-of-2-frame-confirmed + occupancy-gated + cooled-down) into the PHYSICAL axis: BLE-presence and
# device-free motion above can say "something is moving with no known cause" but neither can say
# WHO — a visual, identity-bearing confirmation is the strongest PHYSICAL signal available and BLE/
# motion alone can't produce it (2026-07-08 gen-pair: mesh-perimeter × mesh-face-recognize).
# READ-ONLY on stranger-watch's own state file (no live vision call here — that would double the
# Groq quota burn on top of stranger-watch's own already-tight vision budget gate). Pure (no I/O)
# so --test dry-runs it. Honest-fusion contract:
#   - Only a FRESH, fully-confirmed stranger read (ANNOUNCED or STRANGER-HELD — both mean the 2-of-2
#     frame confirmation already passed inside stranger-watch; HELD differs only by voice-cooldown,
#     not by confidence) raises severity, and it goes straight to ALERT — a visually-identified
#     unknown person is a stronger signal than the device-free-motion NOTICE above.
#   - A stale/missing/non-stranger state line (DARK, SILENT no-vision, SILENT who=Ilya/Rozalia/
#     Nobody, unconfirmed) NEVER escalates — an unconfirmed or absent eye must not manufacture an
#     intruder, and identifying a KNOWN household member is neutral, not corroborating evidence
#     either way (asymmetric: this axis only adds confirmed-stranger signal, never a "camera saw
#     nobody so motion is fine" pass — that would silently override a real BLE/motion NOTICE).
#   - Never LOWERS an existing verdict (same asymmetry as motion_phy_sev/gw_net_sev).
#   - UNREACHABLE→ALERT is intended: a fresh confirmed-stranger read makes a BLE-blind axis assessable.
stranger_phy_sev() {
  local label="$1" fresh="$2" cur="$3"
  case "$label" in
    ANNOUNCED|STRANGER-HELD) [ "$fresh" = 1 ] && { echo "ALERT"; return; } ;;
  esac
  echo "$cur"
}

# stranger_log_scan <state-file> <now_epoch> [fresh_s=600] → sets globals sw_label (ANNOUNCED |
# STRANGER-HELD | other-verbatim-token | "" if unparseable) / sw_fresh (1 if the state's own ts=
# field is within fresh_s of now). fresh_s defaults to 2x stranger-watch's own */5 cadence, same
# convention as gw_log_scan's fresh_s = 2x the canary's */10 stride. File-bound + epoch-parameterized
# so --test drives it on fixtures without touching the live camera/vision path.
stranger_log_scan() {
  local f="$1" now="$2" fresh_s="${3:-600}" line ts ep
  sw_label=""; sw_fresh=0
  [ -r "$f" ] || return 0
  line="$(tail -1 "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$line" ] || return 0
  sw_label="$(printf '%s\n' "$line" | awk '{print $2}')"
  ts="$(printf '%s\n' "$line" | grep -oE 'ts=[0-9TZ:.-]+' | cut -d= -f2)"
  ep=""
  [ -n "$ts" ] && ep="$(date -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$ep" ] && [ $(( now - ep )) -le "$fresh_s" ] && sw_fresh=1
  return 0
}

# pwrbtn_phy_sev <press_recent:0|1> <op_home_label> <op_fresh:0|1> <cur_phy_sev> → echoes the
# (possibly-raised) PHYSICAL severity. Folds mesh-powerbtn (ACPI power-button IRQ counter — fires
# only on a REAL chassis press) into the PHYSICAL axis: BLE/motion/camera above sense the ROOM;
# none senses the chassis itself being touched. Pure (no I/O) so --test dry-runs it. Honest-fusion
# contract (2026-07-14 coevolution-MCC gen-pair: mesh-perimeter × mesh-powerbtn):
#   - Escalates ONLY to NOTICE (a press is touch evidence, not identity), and ONLY when the press
#     is RECENT and a FRESH mesh-operator-home read says AWAY — the chassis was touched while the
#     operator confirmedly wasn't there. That conjunction is the investigate signal.
#   - HOME (routine suspend/wake press) and UNCERTAIN/stale/missing operator-home NEVER escalate —
#     an unknown operator must not manufacture an intruder, and the operator's own daily presses
#     must not cry wolf.
#   - Never LOWERS an existing ALERT. UNREACHABLE→NOTICE intended (a real press makes a blind axis
#     assessable — same asymmetry as motion_phy_sev).
pwrbtn_phy_sev() {
  local recent="$1" oph="$2" fresh="$3" cur="$4"
  if [ "$recent" = 1 ] && [ "$fresh" = 1 ] && [ "$oph" = AWAY ] && [ "$cur" != ALERT ]; then
    echo "NOTICE"; return
  fi
  echo "$cur"
}

# pwrbtn_log_scan <edge-log> <now_epoch> [recent_s=1800] → sets globals pb_recent (1 if the newest
# '[powerbtn] PRESSED' line's own @-timestamp is within recent_s of now) / pb_age (seconds since
# that press; "" if no parseable press). READ-ONLY on the */5 --edge cron's log sink — running
# mesh-powerbtn here would consume the cron's delta baseline (the counter is single-consumer).
# File-bound + epoch-parameterized so --test drives it on fixtures without the live counter.
pwrbtn_log_scan() {
  local f="$1" now="$2" recent_s="${3:-1800}" line ts ep
  pb_recent=0; pb_age=""
  [ -r "$f" ] || return 0
  line="$(grep -F '[powerbtn] PRESSED' "$f" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
  [ -n "$line" ] || return 0
  ts="$(printf '%s\n' "$line" | grep -oE '@ [0-9TZ:-]+' | cut -d' ' -f2)"
  ep=""
  [ -n "$ts" ] && ep="$(date -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$ep" ] || return 0
  pb_age=$(( now - ep ))
  [ "$pb_age" -ge 0 ] && [ "$pb_age" -le "$recent_s" ] && pb_recent=1
  return 0
}

# scape_phy_sev <ear_live:0|1> <impulse:0|1> <op_home_label> <op_fresh:0|1> <cur_phy_sev> → echoes the
# (possibly-raised) PHYSICAL severity. Folds the ROOM EAR into the PHYSICAL axis (2026-07-24
# coevolution gen-pair: mesh-perimeter × mesh-soundscape). Pure (no I/O) so --test dry-runs it.
#
# WHY sound: perimeter's four physical contributors are BLE (needs a carried device), wifi-drift
# motion (device-free but mute about WHAT), the camera (needs light and a frame) and the chassis IRQ
# (needs a touch). None of them HEARS. Sound is the only physical channel that works in the dark,
# around a corner, through a wall, with no device on the intruder — and the mesh already listens:
# mesh-soundscape scans the room mic every 10 min. It measures every window and then THROWS AWAY
# exactly the class a perimeter wants: its grind lane REJECTS windows tagged `(transient <d>b/s — a
# bang, not a rhythm)` / `(beatless <n>b — not grindable)` — a loud window with no rhythm, i.e. a
# door, a drop, a knock. The grinder's reject reason IS the perimeter's signal; same measure tract,
# opposite sign. No new capture, no second librosa analyzer, no double-writer on the ear.
#
# Honest-fusion contract:
#   - NOTICE only, never ALERT: a bang is an event, not an identity (weaker evidence than the
#     2-of-2 camera confirmation, same tier as the chassis press).
#   - Requires a FRESH mesh-operator-home AWAY. HOME never escalates — a person at home makes bangs
#     all day. UNCERTAIN/stale/missing never escalates (an unknown operator must not manufacture an
#     intruder).
#   - Requires ear_live (the scanner's own tick is fresh). A dead/absent ear reads UNKNOWN and
#     escalates nothing — and, crucially, its silence is NOT read as a quiet room: the log is
#     change-gated (quiet windows write no line), so absence of an impulse line means "quiet" ONLY
#     while the tick says the ear ran. Dead ear + no line would otherwise be a faked all-clear.
#   - Never LOWERS an existing ALERT. UNREACHABLE→NOTICE intended (a real bang makes a blind axis
#     assessable — same asymmetry as motion_phy_sev/pwrbtn_phy_sev).
# HONEST LIMIT (write it down, don't oversell): the ear captures ~45s every 10 min ≈ 7.5% duty
# cycle. This senses a SUSTAINED or REPEATED disturbance in an empty house; a single instantaneous
# event is more likely missed than caught. And it cannot tell a door from a dropped pan or the
# fridge compressor — which is exactly why the ceiling is NOTICE (investigate), not ALERT.
scape_phy_sev() {
  local live="$1" imp="$2" oph="$3" fresh="$4" cur="$5"
  if [ "$live" = 1 ] && [ "$imp" = 1 ] && [ "$fresh" = 1 ] && [ "$oph" = AWAY ] && [ "$cur" != ALERT ]; then
    echo "NOTICE"; return
  fi
  echo "$cur"
}

# scape_log_scan <soundscape-log> <tick-file> <now_epoch> [recent_s=1500] [score_floor=50] → sets
# globals ss_live / ss_impulse / ss_age / ss_score / ss_kind. READ-ONLY on mesh-soundscape's own
# */10 cron sink + its liveness marker — running --scan here would take the mic from the ear (and
# cost a capture + a librosa pass per perimeter cycle).
#   ss_live    1 iff the ear's `.soundscape-tick` mtime is within recent_s (liveness-touch convention:
#              the ear touches it on EVERY scan, including quiet ones, so mtime = ran-live while the
#              log stays change-gated to interesting finds).
#   ss_impulse 1 iff the newest log line within recent_s is a grind-REJECTED loud window: score
#              >= score_floor AND tagged `(transient …b/s` or `(beatless …b`.
# recent_s defaults to 1500s (25 min) — must EXCEED the ear's */10 cadence (lease>cadence), so a
# single skipped scan never reads as a dead ear.
scape_log_scan() {
  local f="$1" tick="$2" now="$3" recent_s="${4:-1500}" floor="${5:-50}" line ts ep sc mt
  ss_live=0; ss_impulse=0; ss_age=""; ss_score=""; ss_kind=""
  mt="$(stat -c %Y "$tick" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  [ "$mt" -gt 0 ] && [ $(( now - mt )) -le "$recent_s" ] && ss_live=1
  [ -r "$f" ] || return 0
  # newest scored line, whatever its verdict — an OLDER impulse must not outrank a NEWER calm window
  line="$(grep -E '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}Z  score ' "$f" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
  [ -n "$line" ] || return 0
  ts="$(printf '%s\n' "$line" | awk '{print $1}')"
  ep=""
  [ -n "$ts" ] && ep="$(date -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$ep" ] || return 0
  ss_age=$(( now - ep ))
  { [ "$ss_age" -ge 0 ] && [ "$ss_age" -le "$recent_s" ]; } || { ss_age=""; return 0; }
  sc="$(printf '%s\n' "$line" | awk '{print $3}')"
  case "$sc" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ss_age=""; return 0;; esac
  ss_score="$sc"
  case "$line" in
    *'(transient '*) ss_kind=transient ;;
    *'(beatless '*)  ss_kind=beatless ;;
    *) return 0 ;;
  esac
  [ "$ss_score" -ge "$floor" ] && ss_impulse=1
  return 0
}

# The NETWORK axis's read of its callee, under an EXPLICIT budget. Factored out of the axis block
# below solely so --test can drive the REAL mesh-lan-newdevice at a starved and at the shipped
# budget and watch the coupling directly — a source-text check on the number would assert nothing.
lan_status_read(){
  timeout "${PERIMETER_LAN_TIMEOUT_S:-30}" mesh-lan-newdevice --status 2>/dev/null
}

if [ "${1:-}" = --test ]; then
  command -v ssh >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no ssh)"; exit 1; }
  for t in mesh-watchtower mesh-lan-newdevice mesh-presence; do
    command -v "$t" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no $t)"; exit 1; }
  done
  # Dry-run the honest-fusion contract with synthetic axes (no network) — the core verdict logic the
  # old smoke never touched. A regression flipping worst-of-axes, or silently lowering an unreachable
  # axis to CALM, or faking a CALM when all axes are down, was previously invisible to --test.
  _f=0
  fuse_posture ALERT  CALM   CALM;   { [ "$verdict" = ALERT ]  && [ "$ec" = 0 ]; } || { echo "  FAIL: worst-axis→ALERT (got '$verdict' ec=$ec)"; _f=1; }
  fuse_posture CALM   NOTICE CALM;   { [ "$verdict" = NOTICE ] && [ "$ec" = 0 ]; } || { echo "  FAIL: worst-axis→NOTICE (got '$verdict')"; _f=1; }
  fuse_posture CALM   CALM   CALM;   { [ "$verdict" = CALM ]   && [ "$ec" = 0 ]; } || { echo "  FAIL: all-CALM (got '$verdict')"; _f=1; }
  fuse_posture ALERT  UNKNOWN UNKNOWN; { [ "$verdict" = "ALERT (partial — 2/3 axes unreachable)" ] && [ "$ec" = 0 ]; } || { echo "  FAIL: unreachable=coverage-not-severity+partial (got '$verdict')"; _f=1; }
  fuse_posture UNKNOWN UNKNOWN UNKNOWN; case "$verdict" in CANNOT-ASSESS*) [ "$ec" = 2 ] || { echo "  FAIL: all-unreachable ec=2 (got $ec)"; _f=1; };; *) echo "  FAIL: all-unreachable→CANNOT-ASSESS (got '$verdict')"; _f=1;; esac
  ROBUST_PARTIAL=1; fuse_posture CALM UNKNOWN CALM; { [ "$verdict" = "NOTICE (partial — 1/3 axes unreachable)" ] && [ "$ec" = 0 ]; } || { echo "  FAIL: robust partial all-clear→NOTICE (got '$verdict')"; _f=1; }; ROBUST_PARTIAL="${PERIMETER_ROBUST_PARTIAL:-0}"
  # ---- --edge LINE SHAPE: the log's only durable record, and it must be DATABLE -------------------
  # 572 of 572 lines in ~/.mesh/perimeter.log carried no timestamp until 2026-08-22, so a fired
  # [perimeter-alert] from today was indistinguishable from one in July. The cron wiring is a bare
  # `>>` append of stdout (reflexes.cron:84): what edge_line omits is unrecoverable downstream.
  # Drive the REAL emitter, never a grep for the format string — a source match proves a string is
  # present, not that this code runs.
  _el="$(edge_line '2026-08-22T15:36:47Z' perimeter-alert testhost ALERT CALM UNREACHABLE 'reason text')"
  printf '%s' "$_el" | grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}Z ' \
    || { echo "  FAIL: --edge line does not START with an ISO ts (got '$_el') — an undated alert is undatable"; _f=1; }
  # the ts must be the tool's own, not a literal: a hardcoded date would satisfy the shape check above.
  case "$_el" in '2026-08-22T15:36:47Z '*) : ;; *) echo "  FAIL: edge_line ignored the ts it was passed"; _f=1;; esac
  _el2="$(edge_line '2011-11-11T11:11:11Z' perimeter-calm testhost CALM CALM CALM r)"
  case "$_el2" in '2011-11-11T11:11:11Z '*) : ;; *) echo "  FAIL: edge_line hardcodes a ts instead of using its argument"; _f=1;; esac
  # ORDER IS THE CONTRACT, not taste: the only consumer (mesh-sensor-tape extract_token) strips
  # `^<iso-ts>\s+\S+\s+` before reading the state token, so ts-then-bracket is eaten and the token
  # still resolves. Assert the bracket follows the ts immediately — bracket-first would survive the
  # shape check above only if the ts moved, and ts-then-something-else would eat the wrong word.
  printf '%s' "$_el" | grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9:]+Z \[perimeter-alert\] ' \
    || { echo "  FAIL: mark bracket must follow the ts immediately (mesh-sensor-tape strips ts+next-token)"; _f=1; }
  # and the reader's own strip, applied to this exact line, must still yield the state token.
  _elr="$(printf '%s' "$_el" | sed -E 's/^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9:]+Z[[:space:]]+[^[:space:]]+[[:space:]]+//; s/^[A-Za-z0-9._-]*[a-z][A-Za-z0-9._-]*:[[:space:]]+//' | grep -oE '[A-Z][A-Z0-9_-]{1,}[A-Z0-9]' | head -1)"
  [ "$_elr" = ALERT ] || { echo "  FAIL: mesh-sensor-tape's strip yields '$_elr' on the dated line, want ALERT"; _f=1; }
  # motion-attribution → PHYSICAL severity (the 2026-06-24 motion×operator-home integration)
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev UNEXPLAINED  0 CALM)"        = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: UNEXPLAINED(rc0) raises CALM→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev UNEXPLAINED  0 UNREACHABLE)" = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: UNEXPLAINED(rc0) makes BLE-blind axis assessable→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev UNEXPLAINED  0 ALERT)"       = ALERT ]       || { echo "  FAIL: motion NEVER lowers an existing ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev UNEXPLAINED  2 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: degraded motion (rc2) NEVER escalates (honest-fusion: dead radar ≠ intruder)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev OCCUPANT     0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: OCCUPANT (operator home, benign) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev UNATTRIBUTED 0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: UNATTRIBUTED (op-home unknown, neutral) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(motion_phy_sev QUIET        0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: QUIET (no motion) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  # gateway-identity → NETWORK severity (the 2026-07-03 ARP-canary integration)
  [ "$(gw_net_sev CHANGED 1 1 CALM)"        = ALERT ]       || { echo "  FAIL: fresh gateway-MAC flip raises CALM→ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev CHANGED 1 1 UNREACHABLE)" = ALERT ]       || { echo "  FAIL: fresh flip makes a DHCP-blind axis assessable→ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev STABLE  1 1 CALM)"        = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: rebaselined flip within window raises CALM→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev STABLE  1 1 ALERT)"       = ALERT ]       || { echo "  FAIL: canary NEVER lowers an existing ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev STABLE  1 0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: fresh STABLE binding never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev NEW     1 0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: NEW (first obs / gw moved networks) is neutral"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev ''      0 0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: stale/missing canary log never escalates (dead canary ≠ MITM)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(gw_net_sev ''      0 0 UNREACHABLE)" = UNREACHABLE ] || { echo "  FAIL: stale canary must not fake assessability (blind stays blind)"; _f=1; }
  # gw_log_scan on fixtures (file-bound, epoch-parameterized — no live canary needed)
  _gtd="$(mktemp -d)"; _gl="$_gtd/gw.log"; _gnow=1000000000
  _gts() { date -u -d "@$1" +%FT%TZ; }
  printf '[gateway-identity] STABLE gw=192.168.8.1 mac=aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff dev=wlan0 since=x @ host %s\n' "$(_gts $((_gnow-300)))" > "$_gl"
  gw_log_scan "$_gl" "$_gnow"
  { [ "$gw_label" = STABLE ] && [ "$gw_fresh" = 1 ] && [ "$gw_recent" = 0 ] && [ "$gw_mac" = aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: fresh STABLE line should parse label/mac fresh=1 recent=0 (got $gw_label/$gw_mac/$gw_fresh/$gw_recent)"; _f=1; }
  printf '[gateway-identity] CHANGED gw=192.168.8.1 mac=de:ad:be:ef:00:01 dev=wlan0 was=aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff since=x @ host %s\n' "$(_gts $((_gnow-120)))" >> "$_gl"
  gw_log_scan "$_gl" "$_gnow"
  { [ "$gw_label" = CHANGED ] && [ "$gw_fresh" = 1 ] && [ "$gw_recent" = 1 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: newest fresh CHANGED should set label=CHANGED fresh=1 recent=1 (got $gw_label/$gw_fresh/$gw_recent)"; _f=1; }
  printf '[gateway-identity] STABLE gw=192.168.8.1 mac=de:ad:be:ef:00:01 dev=wlan0 since=x @ host %s\n' "$(_gts $((_gnow-60)))" >> "$_gl"
  gw_log_scan "$_gl" "$_gnow"
  { [ "$gw_label" = STABLE ] && [ "$gw_recent" = 1 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: rebaselined flip (CHANGED then STABLE) must keep recent=1 (got $gw_label/$gw_recent)"; _f=1; }
  gw_log_scan "$_gl" $((_gnow + 7200))
  { [ "$gw_fresh" = 0 ] && [ "$gw_recent" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: 2h-old log must read fresh=0 recent=0 (stale canary, got $gw_fresh/$gw_recent)"; _f=1; }
  printf 'gateway-identity: UNREACHABLE — no default route\n' > "$_gl"
  gw_log_scan "$_gl" "$_gnow"
  { [ -z "$gw_label" ] && [ "$gw_fresh" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: exit-2 stderr lines in the cron log must not parse as a verdict (got '$gw_label')"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_gtd"
  # stranger-watch → PHYSICAL severity (2026-07-08 mesh-perimeter × mesh-face-recognize gen-pair)
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev ANNOUNCED      1 CALM)"  = ALERT ] || { echo "  FAIL: fresh 2-of-2 ANNOUNCED stranger raises CALM→ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev STRANGER-HELD  1 NOTICE)" = ALERT ] || { echo "  FAIL: fresh cooldown-held confirmed stranger raises NOTICE→ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev ANNOUNCED      1 UNREACHABLE)" = ALERT ] || { echo "  FAIL: fresh confirmed stranger makes a BLE-blind axis assessable→ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev ANNOUNCED      0 CALM)"  = CALM ]  || { echo "  FAIL: stale confirmed-stranger line (fresh=0) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev SILENT         1 CALM)"  = CALM ]  || { echo "  FAIL: unconfirmed/known-person SILENT never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev DARK           1 CALM)"  = CALM ]  || { echo "  FAIL: DARK (no fresh camera frame) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(stranger_phy_sev ''             0 NOTICE)" = NOTICE ] || { echo "  FAIL: missing/unparseable state never escalates"; _f=1; }
  # stranger_log_scan on fixtures (file-bound, epoch-parameterized — no live camera/vision needed)
  _std="$(mktemp -d)"; _sl="$_std/stranger.state"; _snow=1000000000
  _sts() { date -u -d "@$1" +%FT%TZ; }
  printf '[stranger-watch] SILENT who=Ilya occupancy=OCCUPIED ts=%s\n' "$(_sts $((_snow-30)))" > "$_sl"
  stranger_log_scan "$_sl" "$_snow"
  { [ "$sw_label" = SILENT ] && [ "$sw_fresh" = 1 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: fresh known-person SILENT line should parse label=SILENT fresh=1 (got $sw_label/$sw_fresh)"; _f=1; }
  printf '[stranger-watch] ANNOUNCED stranger 2-of-2 occupancy=OCCUPIED ts=%s\n' "$(_sts $((_snow-30)))" >> "$_sl"
  stranger_log_scan "$_sl" "$_snow"
  { [ "$sw_label" = ANNOUNCED ] && [ "$sw_fresh" = 1 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: fresh ANNOUNCED line should parse label=ANNOUNCED fresh=1 (got $sw_label/$sw_fresh)"; _f=1; }
  stranger_log_scan "$_sl" $((_snow + 7200))
  { [ "$sw_fresh" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: 2h-old state must read fresh=0 (stale eye, got $sw_fresh)"; _f=1; }
  printf 'not a real state line at all\n' > "$_sl"
  stranger_log_scan "$_sl" "$_snow"
  { [ "$sw_fresh" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: unparseable ts must not fake freshness (got fresh=$sw_fresh)"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_std"
  # powerbtn → PHYSICAL severity (2026-07-14 coevolution-MCC gen-pair: perimeter × mesh-powerbtn)
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 1 AWAY      1 CALM)"        = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: recent press + fresh AWAY raises CALM→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 1 AWAY      1 UNREACHABLE)" = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: press+AWAY makes a blind axis assessable→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 1 AWAY      1 ALERT)"       = ALERT ]       || { echo "  FAIL: powerbtn NEVER lowers an existing ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 1 HOME      1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: press while HOME (routine suspend/wake) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 1 UNCERTAIN 1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: press with UNCERTAIN op-home never escalates (unknown ≠ intruder)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 1 AWAY      0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: press with STALE op-home never escalates (dead sensor ≠ intruder)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(pwrbtn_phy_sev 0 AWAY      1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: no recent press never escalates"; _f=1; }
  # pwrbtn_log_scan on fixtures (file-bound, epoch-parameterized — no live IRQ counter needed)
  _ptd="$(mktemp -d)"; _pl="$_ptd/powerbtn.log"; _pnow=1000000000
  _pts() { date -u -d "@$1" +%FT%TZ; }
  printf '[powerbtn] PRESSED — 2 press(es) since last check (total=6) @ %s\n' "$(_pts $((_pnow-300)))" > "$_pl"
  pwrbtn_log_scan "$_pl" "$_pnow"
  { [ "$pb_recent" = 1 ] && [ "$pb_age" = 300 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: 5-min-old PRESSED line should read recent=1 age=300 (got $pb_recent/$pb_age)"; _f=1; }
  pwrbtn_log_scan "$_pl" $((_pnow + 7200))
  { [ "$pb_recent" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: 2h-old press must read recent=0 (got $pb_recent)"; _f=1; }
  printf '[powerbtn] QUIET — delta=0 total=6 @ %s\n' "$(_pts $((_pnow-60)))" > "$_pl"
  pwrbtn_log_scan "$_pl" "$_pnow"
  { [ "$pb_recent" = 0 ] && [ -z "$pb_age" ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: QUIET-only log must not read as a press (got $pb_recent/$pb_age)"; _f=1; }
  printf 'garbage without a timestamp\n' > "$_pl"
  pwrbtn_log_scan "$_pl" "$_pnow"
  { [ "$pb_recent" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: unparseable log must not fake a recent press (got $pb_recent)"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_ptd"
  # room ear → PHYSICAL severity (2026-07-24 coevolution gen-pair: perimeter × mesh-soundscape)
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 1 AWAY      1 CALM)"        = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: fresh impulse + fresh AWAY raises CALM→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 1 AWAY      1 UNREACHABLE)" = NOTICE ]      || { echo "  FAIL: impulse+AWAY makes a blind axis assessable→NOTICE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 1 AWAY      1 ALERT)"       = ALERT ]       || { echo "  FAIL: the ear NEVER lowers an existing ALERT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 1 HOME      1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: a bang while HOME (the operator lives here) never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 1 UNCERTAIN 1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: impulse with UNCERTAIN op-home never escalates"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 1 AWAY      0 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: impulse with STALE op-home never escalates (dead sensor ≠ intruder)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 0 1 AWAY      1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: a DEAD ear never escalates (its silence is not evidence)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(scape_phy_sev 1 0 AWAY      1 CALM)"        = CALM ]        || { echo "  FAIL: live ear, no impulse → quiet house stays CALM"; _f=1; }
  # scape_log_scan on fixtures (file-bound, epoch-parameterized — no mic, no librosa, no live ear)
  _std2="$(mktemp -d)"; _sl2="$_std2/soundscape.log"; _tk="$_std2/tick"; _snow2=1000000000
  _sts() { date -u -d "@$1" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ; }
  touch -d "@$((_snow2-300))" "$_tk"
  printf '%s  score 58 1b  [dynamic·dark·tonal]  win 3.00s@30.00s  -> (beatless 1b — not grindable)\n' "$(_sts $((_snow2-300)))" > "$_sl2"
  scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_tk" "$_snow2"
  { [ "$ss_live" = 1 ] && [ "$ss_impulse" = 1 ] && [ "$ss_kind" = beatless ] && [ "$ss_score" = 58 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: fresh loud beatless window → live=1 impulse=1 (got $ss_live/$ss_impulse/$ss_kind/$ss_score)"; _f=1; }
  scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_tk" $((_snow2 + 7200))
  { [ "$ss_live" = 0 ] && [ "$ss_impulse" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: 2h-old tick+line must read live=0 impulse=0 (got $ss_live/$ss_impulse)"; _f=1; }
  printf '%s  score 61 12b  [even·bright·tonal]  win 12.00s@0.00s  -> grinding (bg, ≤240s)\n' "$(_sts $((_snow2-300)))" > "$_sl2"
  scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_tk" "$_snow2"
  { [ "$ss_live" = 1 ] && [ "$ss_impulse" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: a RHYTHMIC loud window is music/speech, not a bang — impulse must stay 0 (got $ss_impulse)"; _f=1; }
  printf '%s  score 46 2b  [even·dark·tonal]  win 3.00s@3.00s  -> (beatless 2b — not grindable)\n' "$(_sts $((_snow2-300)))" > "$_sl2"
  scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_tk" "$_snow2"
  { [ "$ss_impulse" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: a QUIET beatless window (score<floor) is room tone, not a bang (got impulse=$ss_impulse)"; _f=1; }
  printf '%s  score 58 1b  [x]  win 3.00s@30.00s  -> (beatless 1b — not grindable)\n' "$(_sts $((_snow2-300)))"  > "$_sl2"
  printf '%s  score 52 9b  [x]  win 12.00s@0.00s  -> grinding (bg, ≤240s)\n'          "$(_sts $((_snow2-120)))" >> "$_sl2"
  scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_tk" "$_snow2"
  { [ "$ss_impulse" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: an OLDER impulse must not outrank the NEWEST scored window (got impulse=$ss_impulse)"; _f=1; }
  printf 'garbage without a timestamp\n' > "$_sl2"
  scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_tk" "$_snow2"
  { [ "$ss_impulse" = 0 ] && [ -z "$ss_age" ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: unparseable log must not fake an impulse (got $ss_impulse/$ss_age)"; _f=1; }
  rm -f "$_sl2"; scape_log_scan "$_sl2" "$_std2/absent-tick" "$_snow2"
  { [ "$ss_live" = 0 ] && [ "$ss_impulse" = 0 ]; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL: a node with NO ear at all must read live=0 impulse=0, never a quiet all-clear"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_std2"
  # ---- BUDGET-vs-CALLEE coupling (the live axis, not a fixture) --------------------------------
  # A budget under the callee's worst case does not merely blank this axis — the SIGTERM lands
  # before mesh-lan-newdevice touches its liveness beat, so ONE under-budget yields a blind NETWORK
  # and a [reflex-stale] on a sense that was answering. MESH_LAN_BEAT redirects the callee's beat to
  # a temp file so this dry-run can never forge the beat mesh-reflex-health ages (09f7914).
  _bt="$(mktemp -d)"; _leases(){ awk 'NF>=3 && $3 ~ /^[0-9a-fA-F:]{17}$/ {n++} END{exit !(n>0)}'; }
  # (a) ARITHMETIC — the shipped budget must cover the callee's OWN declared caps, which the latency
  # leg below cannot prove: a fast moment passes even an under-provisioned budget. Read from the
  # CALLEE's source, so this is not a self-matching grep; an unparseable read FAILS rather than
  # passing quietly, so a callee refactor surfaces here instead of re-opening the hole silently.
  _callee="$(command -v mesh-lan-newdevice)"
  _dhcp_s="$(grep -oE 'leases="\$\(timeout ([0-9]+) ' "$_callee" | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1)"
  _arp_s="$(grep -oE 'MESH_LAN_ARP_SWEEP_S:-([0-9]+)' "$_callee" | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1)"
  if [ -n "$_dhcp_s" ] && [ -n "$_arp_s" ]; then
    _need=$(( _dhcp_s + _arp_s ))
    [ "${PERIMETER_LAN_TIMEOUT_S:-30}" -ge "$_need" ] \
      || { echo "  FAIL: NETWORK budget ${PERIMETER_LAN_TIMEOUT_S:-30}s < mesh-lan-newdevice's own worst case ${_dhcp_s}+${_arp_s}=${_need}s — the axis will read UNREACHABLE and skip the callee's beat on a slow LAN"; _f=1; }
  else
    echo "  FAIL: could not read mesh-lan-newdevice's own timeout caps (dhcp='$_dhcp_s' arp='$_arp_s') — the budget invariant is unverifiable, not satisfied"; _f=1
  fi
  # (b) LATENCY — drive the REAL callee. The 60s probe establishes whether this node's LAN is
  # observable AT ALL, so an unobservable-LAN skip can never absorb an under-provisioned budget:
  # readable at 60s but not at the shipped budget is a FAIL, not a skip.
  _gen="$(export MESH_LAN_BEAT="$_bt/gen" PERIMETER_LAN_TIMEOUT_S=60; lan_status_read)"
  if printf '%s\n' "$_gen" | _leases; then
    _real="$(export MESH_LAN_BEAT="$_bt/real"; lan_status_read)"                  # shipped budget
    printf '%s\n' "$_real" | _leases \
      || { echo "  FAIL: LAN reads leases at 60s but NOT at the shipped ${PERIMETER_LAN_TIMEOUT_S:-30}s budget — the axis is blind by its own timeout, not by the LAN"; _f=1; }
    [ -f "$_bt/real" ] || { echo "  FAIL: shipped budget read leases but the callee's beat was never touched"; _f=1; }
    _starved="$(export MESH_LAN_BEAT="$_bt/starved" PERIMETER_LAN_TIMEOUT_S=1; lan_status_read)"
    [ -z "$_starved" ] || { echo "  FAIL: a 1s budget must KILL the callee, not return a status (got ${#_starved} bytes) — the starve leg is vacuous"; _f=1; }
    [ -f "$_bt/starved" ] && { echo "  FAIL: a killed callee must NOT have touched its liveness beat"; _f=1; }
  else
    echo "  note: budget-LATENCY leg SKIPPED — the callee reads no leases even at 60s, so this node's LAN is unobservable and no budget can be exonerated by it (the ARITHMETIC leg above still binds)"
  fi
  rm -rf "$_bt"; unset -f _leases
  [ "$_f" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (fusion contract)"; exit 1; }
  # at least one axis must be reachable or there is nothing live to fuse (the logic above is proven regardless)
  if mesh-watchtower --test >/dev/null 2>&1 || mesh-lan-newdevice --test >/dev/null 2>&1 \
     || mesh-presence --test >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "smoke-test: ok (fusion contract: 68 assertions [6 fuse + 5 edge-line-shape (dated, ts-is-the-argument, bracket-follows-ts, reader-strip-still-resolves) + 7 motion→PHYSICAL + 8 gw→NETWORK + 5 gw-log-scan + 7 stranger→PHYSICAL + 4 stranger-log-scan + 7 pwrbtn→PHYSICAL + 4 pwrbtn-log-scan + 8 scape→PHYSICAL + 7 scape-log-scan] + 5 budget-vs-callee [1 arithmetic, unconditional + 4 latency, gated on an observable LAN] + ≥1 axis reachable)"; exit 0
  fi
  echo "smoke-test: n/a (fusion contract: 63 assertions + budget-vs-callee PASS; no axis reachable now — outside/network/physical all down)"; exit 2
fi

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --multiverse — SPECIFICATION-CURVE / MULTIVERSE audit of our own fusion recipe.
# (added 2026-08-22; review: docs/reviews/allostatic-load-specification-curve-panel-vs-rule-perimeter-2026-08-22.md)
#
# Patel, P. C. (2026), "Biomarker Panel Selection Explains Heterogeneity in Allostatic Load-Mortality
# Risk: A Specification-Curve Analysis", medRxiv 2026.05.06.26352579, doi:10.64898/2026.05.06.26352579,
# is the live form of the standing critique of allostatic load: the field uses "at least 18 distinct
# calculation methods across 26 biomarkers", so a single index cannot say whether its number is a
# signal or "an artifact of investigator choice". The answer is not a better index — it is to
# ENUMERATE the defensible alternatives, report the DISTRIBUTION of outcomes, and attribute the
# between-specification variance to the choice that drove it. Patel's headline: panel composition
# explained 46% of it, covariate adjustment 4%.
#
# This verdict is an allostatic-load index in everything but name: a hand-picked PANEL of axes reduced
# by ONE hand-picked SCORING RULE (worst reachable) under ONE coverage config (ROBUST_PARTIAL) into a
# single band. Nothing here had ever asked whether the band survives a different defensible recipe.
# So: replay the durable edge log across panel (7 non-empty subsets) x rule (3) x partial (2) = 42
# specifications, and report the curve plus which factor moves it.
#
# Read-only: it raises no probe and touches no state. It does NOT change the shipped verdict — the
# point is to publish how contingent that verdict is, not to pick a different one.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ "${1:-}" = --multiverse ]; then
  PERIM_LOG="${PERIMETER_LOG:-$HOME/.mesh/perimeter.log}"
  python3 - "$PERIM_LOG" <<'PY'
import re, sys, itertools
from statistics import mean

path = sys.argv[1]
rows = []
try:
    with open(path, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as fh:
        for line in fh:
            m = re.search(r'O=(\S+) N=(\S+) P=(\S+)', line)
            if m:
                rows.append(m.groups())
except FileNotFoundError:
    print('n=0 coverage=none (no %s — nothing to replay)' % path); raise SystemExit(2)

n = len(rows)
if n == 0:
    print('n=0 coverage=none (log has no O=/N=/P= lines)'); raise SystemExit(2)

RANK = {'ALERT': 3, 'NOTICE': 2, 'CALM': 1}
BAND = {3: 'ALERT', 2: 'NOTICE', 1: 'CALM'}
IDX = {'O': 0, 'N': 1, 'P': 2}

def verdict(row, panel, rule, partial):
    sev = [RANK.get(row[IDX[a]], 0) for a in panel]
    reach = [x for x in sev if x > 0]
    unreach = len(sev) - len(reach)
    if not reach:
        return 'CANNOT-ASSESS'          # coverage, never a silent CALM — same contract as fuse_posture
    if rule == 'worst':                 # SHIPPED: non-compensatory max over reachable axes
        v = BAND[max(reach)]
    elif rule == 'count':               # the classic allostatic-load quartile-COUNT algorithm
        k = sum(1 for x in reach if x >= 2)
        v = 'ALERT' if k >= 2 else ('NOTICE' if k == 1 else 'CALM')
    else:                               # 'mean' — a compensatory average, the third defensible rule
        v = BAND[max(1, min(3, int(round(mean(reach)))))]
    if partial and v == 'CALM' and unreach > 0:
        v = 'NOTICE'
    return v

panels = [p for r in (1, 2, 3) for p in itertools.combinations('ONP', r)]
rules = ['worst', 'count', 'mean']
parts = [0, 1]
specs = [(p, r, q) for p in panels for r in rules for q in parts]
res = {sp: [verdict(row, *sp) for row in rows] for sp in specs}

SHIPPED = (('O', 'N', 'P'), 'worst', 0)   # panel=ONP, rule=worst-reachable, ROBUST_PARTIAL default 0

def elev(v):
    return sum(1 for x in v if x in ('NOTICE', 'ALERT')) / n * 100

def disagree(a, b):
    return sum(1 for x, y in zip(res[a], res[b]) if x != y) / n

es = sorted(elev(v) for v in res.values())
ag = sorted(sum(1 for x, y in zip(res[SHIPPED], v) if x == y) / n * 100
            for sp, v in res.items() if sp != SHIPPED)

print('n=%d specs=%d (panel 7 x rule 3 x partial 2)' % (n, len(specs)))
print('shipped: panel=ONP rule=worst partial=%s -> elevated=%.1f%% of edges'
      % ('on' if SHIPPED[2] else 'off', elev(res[SHIPPED])))
print('spec-curve elevated%%: min=%.1f p50=%.1f max=%.1f' % (es[0], es[len(es) // 2], es[-1]))
print('agreement-with-shipped%%: min=%.1f p50=%.1f max=%.1f' % (ag[0], ag[len(ag) // 2], ag[-1]))

# LEAVE-ONE-OUT over the panel, holding the shipped rule/config: Patel's "which biomarkers".
for a in 'ONP':
    pan = tuple(x for x in 'ONP' if x != a)
    print('  drop-%s: %.1f%% of edges change verdict' % (a, disagree(SHIPPED, (pan, 'worst', 0)) * 100))

# FACTOR ATTRIBUTION: mean pairwise disagreement between specifications that differ in EXACTLY one
# factor. Not an R^2 and not called one — it is the answer to "which knob moves the verdict".
if n < 30:
    print('attribution: na (n=%d < 30 edges — too few to attribute)' % n)
else:
    att = {}
    for fi, name in ((0, 'panel'), (1, 'rule'), (2, 'partial')):
        ds = [disagree(a, b) for a, b in itertools.combinations(specs, 2)
              if a[fi] != b[fi] and all(a[k] == b[k] for k in range(3) if k != fi)]
        att[name] = mean(ds) if ds else 0.0
    tot = sum(att.values()) or 1.0
    print('attribution: ' + '  '.join('%s=%.0f%% (d=%.3f)' % (k, v / tot * 100, v)
                                      for k, v in att.items()))

# COVERAGE, stated IN the reading: the log is written by --edge, i.e. only when the SHIPPED verdict
# changed. So this is a specification curve over TRANSITIONS, not over time — an alternative spec's
# elevated% is its share of those transitions, never its duty cycle. A spec that would have moved on
# occasions the shipped one sat still is INVISIBLE here, which biases every alternative DOWNWARD.
print('coverage: edge-sampled (shipped-verdict transitions only) — elevated%% is a share of edges, '
      'NOT a duty cycle; alternatives that would fire where the shipped one held still are unseen')
PY
  exit $?
fi

RAW=0; EDGE=0
[ "${1:-}" = --raw ] && RAW=1
[ "${1:-}" = --edge ] && EDGE=1
STATEFILE="$HOME/.mesh/.perimeter.state"
# For --edge: snapshot the PREVIOUS verdict before the scan overwrites the state file
prev_verdict=""
if [ "$EDGE" -eq 1 ] && [ -f "$STATEFILE" ]; then
  prev_verdict="$(awk -F'\t' '{print $5}' "$STATEFILE" 2>/dev/null | head -1 || true)"
fi
HOST="$(hostname)"; TS="$(date -u +%FT%TZ)"
rawfacts=""

# ---- OUTSIDE: phaedra public-IP vantage (mesh-watchtower) ----------------------------------------
out_sev="UNREACHABLE"; out_msg="phaedra vantage unreachable (exit 2) — outside is BLIND, not clear"
wt="$(timeout 35 mesh-watchtower 2>/dev/null)"; wtrc=$?
if [ $wtrc -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$wt" ]; then
  rawfacts+="OUTSIDE	$wt"$'\n'
  if printf '%s' "$wt" | grep -q 'reach-down'; then
    out_sev="ALERT"; out_msg="external REACH DOWN — mesh blind from the outside: $wt"
  elif printf '%s' "$wt" | grep -q 'SCAN-SPIKE'; then
    # known-benign suppression: fingerprint the scan pattern (exposed ports + key-only auth).
    # A recurrence of the SAME ports every hour is routine internet noise, not an incident.
    # After BENIGN_SUPPRESS repeats, downgrade to CALM — the first N spikes still surface so
    # a genuinely NEW scan target (new port opened) is caught before suppression kicks in.
    fp="$(printf '%s' "$wt" | grep -oE 'exposes\[[^]]+\]' | head -1)"
    fp="${fp:-SCAN-SPIKE}"
    bcount=0
    if [ -f "$BENIGN_STATE" ]; then bcount=$(grep -m1 -F "$fp" "$BENIGN_STATE" 2>/dev/null | cut -d'|' -f2); fi
    bcount=$(( ${bcount:-0} + 1 ))
    grep -vF "$fp" "$BENIGN_STATE" 2>/dev/null > "$BENIGN_STATE.tmp" ; printf '%s|%d\n' "$fp" "$bcount" >> "$BENIGN_STATE.tmp"; mv -f "$BENIGN_STATE.tmp" "$BENIGN_STATE"
    if [ "$bcount" -ge "$BENIGN_SUPPRESS" ]; then
      out_sev="CALM"; out_msg="scan spike (known-benign ×${bcount}, suppressed): $wt"
    else
      out_sev="NOTICE"; out_msg="scan spike (benign: mesh is key-only auth, no password path): $wt"
    fi
  else
    out_sev="CALM"; out_msg="$wt"
  fi
else
  rawfacts+="OUTSIDE	UNREACHABLE (watchtower rc=$wtrc)"$'\n'
fi

# ---- NETWORK: home-LAN new/unknown device (mesh-lan-newdevice baseline, READ-ONLY) ---------------
net_sev="UNREACHABLE"; net_msg="router DHCP unreachable — LAN is BLIND, not clear"
# BUDGET ≥ the callee's OWN worst case, or this axis kills a healthy sense and calls it blind.
# mesh-lan-newdevice's two legs are each independently capped: `timeout 12` on the router-DHCP ssh
# (mesh-lan-newdevice:326) and `timeout ${MESH_LAN_ARP_SWEEP_S:-12}` on the ARP fallback sweep
# (:191) — they run in SEQUENCE on this node, where the router refuses the key (Permission denied
# (publickey), measured 2026-08-17) so EVERY run pays the ssh leg and then falls through to ARP.
# Worst case is therefore 12+12=24s BY CONSTRUCTION and the old `timeout 20` sat 4s under it: the
# axis flapped UNREACHABLE on 19 of the last 40 log edges while `--status` returned 9-13 leases on
# 8 of 8 hand-runs. Worse, the SIGTERM lands before mesh-lan-newdevice:339 `: > "$BEAT"`, so every
# budget kill ALSO skips that reflex's liveness beat — one under-budget produced both a blind
# security axis and an 8.7h [reflex-stale] on a sense that was answering the whole time. (The
# OUTSIDE axis above already allots 35s to a lighter callee; NETWORK was the outlier.)
st="$(lan_status_read)"
leases="$(printf '%s\n' "$st" | awk 'NF>=3 && $3 ~ /^[0-9a-fA-F:]{17}$/ {print tolower($3)}')"
# Leases-parsed wins: the ARP-fallback status line says "router DHCP unreachable — LAN-side ARP
# fallback" while delivering REAL leases, so a bare 'unreachable' grep over the whole output kept
# this axis dark for days (blind since ~2026-06-29) with 16 live leases in hand. Only a status
# with NO parseable leases is a true UNREACHABLE.
if [ -n "$leases" ]; then
  declare -A KMAC=()
  if [ -f "$KNOWN_LAN" ]; then while read -r m; do [ -n "$m" ] && KMAC["$(printf '%s' "$m" | tr 'A-F' 'a-f')"]=1; done < "$KNOWN_LAN"; fi
  unk_global=""; unk_laa=""; nlease=0
  while read -r mac; do
    [ -n "$mac" ] || continue; nlease=$((nlease+1))
    [ -n "${KMAC[$mac]:-}" ] && continue
    if is_laa "$mac"; then unk_laa+="$mac "; else unk_global+="$mac "; fi
  done <<<"$leases"
  rawfacts+="NETWORK	$nlease leases; unknown-global=[${unk_global% }] unknown-laa=[${unk_laa% }]"$'\n'
  if [ -n "$unk_global" ]; then
    net_sev="ALERT"; net_msg="UNKNOWN device on LAN (globally-unique MAC = real intrusion signal): ${unk_global% }"
  elif [ -n "$unk_laa" ]; then
    net_sev="NOTICE"; net_msg="unknown LAN MAC but randomized (likely a known device rotating): ${unk_laa% }"
  else
    net_sev="CALM"; net_msg="$nlease leases, all known (baseline ${#KMAC[@]} MACs)"
  fi
  printf '%s' "$st" | grep -q '^source: arp' && net_msg+=" [via LAN ARP fallback]"
elif printf '%s' "$st" | grep -qi 'unreachable'; then
  rawfacts+="NETWORK	UNREACHABLE (router DHCP unreadable)"$'\n'
else
  rawfacts+="NETWORK	UNREACHABLE (no leases parsed)"$'\n'
fi

# ---- NETWORK (cont.): gateway ARP-binding canary (mesh-gateway-identity, READ-ONLY log tail) ------
# lan-newdevice above answers WHO is on the LAN; this answers whether the GATEWAY ITSELF is still the
# device it was. READ-ONLY on the canary's own cron log (running it live here would double-write its
# change-gated baseline — the same no-race doctrine as the lan-newdevice READ-ONLY read above).
GW_LOG="${PERIMETER_GW_LOG:-$HOME/.mesh/gateway-identity.log}"
gw_log_scan "$GW_LOG" "$(date +%s)" "${PERIMETER_GW_FRESH:-1200}" "${PERIMETER_GW_RECENT:-1800}"
net_sev_before="$net_sev"
net_sev="$(gw_net_sev "$gw_label" "$gw_fresh" "$gw_recent" "$net_sev")"
if [ "$gw_fresh" = 1 ] || [ "$gw_recent" = 1 ]; then
  rawfacts+="NETWORK-GATEWAY	$gw_label mac=$gw_mac fresh=$gw_fresh recent_changed=$gw_recent"$'\n'
  if [ "$net_sev" != "$net_sev_before" ]; then
    case "$gw_label" in
      CHANGED) net_msg="GATEWAY MAC FLIPPED (ARP-spoof/MITM or router swap; now mac=$gw_mac — verify the router); ${net_msg}" ;;
      *)       net_msg="gateway MAC flipped within the last 30m (canary rebaselined; now $gw_label $gw_mac); ${net_msg}" ;;
    esac
  else
    net_msg="$net_msg; gw=${gw_label}(${gw_mac})"
  fi
else
  rawfacts+="NETWORK-GATEWAY	unattestable (canary log stale/missing) — not escalating (honest-fusion)"$'\n'
fi

# ---- PHYSICAL: BLE devices in radio range (mesh-presence) ----------------------------------------
# BLE is intentionally INFORMATIONAL (the dedicated edge sense is mesh-presence-delta) — a busy
# environment is noisy, so perimeter reports presence without crying wolf. UNREACHABLE/BUSY stay
# distinct from "0 in range".
phy_sev="UNREACHABLE"; phy_msg="BLE adapter unavailable — physical proximity BLIND, not clear"
pr="$(timeout $((BLE_WIN + 40)) mesh-presence "$BLE_WIN" 2>/dev/null)"; prc=$?
if [ $prc -eq 3 ]; then
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL	BUSY (adapter scan-locked by another scanner — not an empty reading)"$'\n'
  phy_sev="UNREACHABLE"; phy_msg="BLE adapter busy (another scanner holds it) — could not sample"
elif [ $prc -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$pr" ]; then
  dev="$(printf '%s\n' "$pr" | grep -E '^-?[0-9]+\|')"
  ndev="$(printf '%s\n' "$dev" | grep -c . )"
  # named = a real name (not '?'), excluding obvious randomized device-name tokens (SC-XXXX hex)
  named="$(printf '%s\n' "$dev" | awk -F'|' '$3!="?" && $3!="" && $3 !~ /^SC-[0-9A-F]+$/ {print $3}' | paste -sd';' -)"
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL	$ndev BLE in range; named=[$named]"$'\n'
  phy_sev="CALM"; phy_msg="$ndev BLE device(s) in range${named:+; named: $named}"
elif [ $prc -eq 1 ] || [ $prc -eq 0 ]; then
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL	0 devices (presence rc=$prc)"$'\n'
  phy_sev="CALM"; phy_msg="0 BLE devices in range"
else
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL	UNREACHABLE (presence rc=$prc)"$'\n'
  phy_msg="BLE adapter could not be read (rc=$prc)"
fi

# ---- PHYSICAL (cont.): MOTION × operator-home (mesh-motion-attribution) -----------------------------
# BLE above sees DEVICES; this adds whether unexplained physical MOTION is happening while the operator
# is AWAY — the device-less intruder BLE-presence alone misses (0 BLE devices is exactly that case).
# Reads its own cached presence.log (no live BLE scan → no adapter contention with the block above);
# its wifi-motion probe is independent. Honest-fusion: ONLY a confident UNEXPLAINED raises severity
# (to NOTICE); a degraded/UNKNOWN read never escalates, OCCUPANT/ATTRIBUTED/UNATTRIBUTED/QUIET don't.
ma_json="$(timeout 30 mesh-motion-attribution --json 2>/dev/null)"; marc=$?
ma_verdict="$(printf '%s' "$ma_json" | sed -nE 's/.*"verdict":"([^"]+)".*/\1/p')"
[ -n "$ma_verdict" ] || ma_verdict="UNKNOWN"
phy_sev_before="$phy_sev"
phy_sev="$(motion_phy_sev "$ma_verdict" "$marc" "$phy_sev")"
if [ "$marc" -eq 0 ]; then
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-MOTION	$ma_verdict"$'\n'
  case "$ma_verdict" in
    UNEXPLAINED)
      [ "$phy_sev" != "$phy_sev_before" ] \
        && phy_msg="$phy_msg; MOTION while operator AWAY, no carried-device cause (UNEXPLAINED — investigate)" \
        || phy_msg="$phy_msg; UNEXPLAINED motion (already $phy_sev_before)" ;;
    OCCUPANT|ATTRIBUTED|UNATTRIBUTED) phy_msg="$phy_msg; motion=$ma_verdict (benign/neutral)" ;;
  esac
else
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-MOTION	UNKNOWN/degraded (motion-attribution rc=$marc) — not escalating (honest-fusion)"$'\n'
fi

# ---- PHYSICAL (cont.): visual identity (mesh-stranger-watch → mesh-face-recognize, READ-ONLY) -------
# Neither BLE presence nor device-free motion above can say WHO is causing a reading; stranger-watch
# already runs a 2-of-2-frame-confirmed, occupancy-gated camera classification on its own cadence —
# this reads its state file only (no live vision call, no extra Groq-quota burn stacked on top of
# stranger-watch's own tight vision budget).
STRANGER_STATE="${PERIMETER_STRANGER_STATE:-$HOME/.mesh/.stranger-watch.state}"
stranger_log_scan "$STRANGER_STATE" "$(date +%s)" "${PERIMETER_STRANGER_FRESH:-600}"
phy_sev_before="$phy_sev"
phy_sev="$(stranger_phy_sev "$sw_label" "$sw_fresh" "$phy_sev")"
if [ -n "$sw_label" ]; then
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-VISION	$sw_label (fresh=$sw_fresh)"$'\n'
  if [ "$phy_sev" != "$phy_sev_before" ]; then
    phy_msg="$phy_msg; VISUAL CONFIRMATION: camera identified a stranger (mesh-stranger-watch $sw_label, fresh)"
  fi
else
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-VISION	unattestable (stranger-watch state stale/missing) — not escalating (honest-fusion)"$'\n'
fi

# ---- PHYSICAL (cont.): chassis touch (mesh-powerbtn edge log × mesh-operator-home, READ-ONLY) -------
# The room senses above can't feel the chassis ITSELF being touched; the ACPI power-button IRQ
# counter can (it fires only on a real press). A recent press while the operator is confirmedly
# AWAY = someone at the box nobody should be at → NOTICE (pwrbtn_phy_sev contract above).
PWRBTN_LOG="${PERIMETER_PWRBTN_LOG:-$HOME/.mesh/powerbtn.log}"
pwrbtn_log_scan "$PWRBTN_LOG" "$(date +%s)" "${PERIMETER_PWRBTN_RECENT:-1800}"
if [ "$pb_recent" = 1 ]; then
  OPHOME_STATE="${PERIMETER_OPHOME_STATE:-$HOME/.mesh/.op-home.state}"
  oph_label=""; oph_fresh=0
  if [ -f "$OPHOME_STATE" ]; then
    oph_label="$(head -1 "$OPHOME_STATE" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '[:space:]')"
    [ $(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$OPHOME_STATE" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) )) -le "${PERIMETER_OPHOME_FRESH:-900}" ] && oph_fresh=1
  fi
  phy_sev_before="$phy_sev"
  phy_sev="$(pwrbtn_phy_sev "$pb_recent" "${oph_label:-UNKNOWN}" "$oph_fresh" "$phy_sev")"
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-POWERBTN	PRESSED ${pb_age}s ago (op-home=${oph_label:-missing} fresh=$oph_fresh)"$'\n'
  if [ "$phy_sev" != "$phy_sev_before" ]; then
    phy_msg="$phy_msg; chassis POWER BUTTON pressed ${pb_age}s ago while operator AWAY (investigate)"
  else
    phy_msg="$phy_msg; power-button press ${pb_age}s ago (op-home=${oph_label:-unknown} — routine/uncorroborated, not escalating)"
  fi
fi

# ---- PHYSICAL (cont.): the ROOM EAR (mesh-soundscape log × mesh-operator-home, READ-ONLY) ----------
# Nothing above HEARS. The ear already scans the mic every 10 min and DISCARDS the grind-rejected
# windows — a loud window with no rhythm is a bang, not music (scape_phy_sev contract above).
SCAPE_LOG="${PERIMETER_SCAPE_LOG:-$HOME/.mesh/soundscape.log}"
SCAPE_TICK="${PERIMETER_SCAPE_TICK:-$HOME/.mesh/.soundscape-tick}"
scape_log_scan "$SCAPE_LOG" "$SCAPE_TICK" "$(date +%s)" \
               "${PERIMETER_SCAPE_RECENT:-1500}" "${PERIMETER_SCAPE_FLOOR:-50}"
if [ "$ss_live" = 1 ]; then
  if [ "$ss_impulse" = 1 ]; then
    OPHOME_STATE="${PERIMETER_OPHOME_STATE:-$HOME/.mesh/.op-home.state}"
    oph_label=""; oph_fresh=0
    if [ -f "$OPHOME_STATE" ]; then
      oph_label="$(head -1 "$OPHOME_STATE" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '[:space:]')"
      [ $(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$OPHOME_STATE" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) )) -le "${PERIMETER_OPHOME_FRESH:-900}" ] && oph_fresh=1
    fi
    phy_sev_before="$phy_sev"
    phy_sev="$(scape_phy_sev "$ss_live" "$ss_impulse" "${oph_label:-UNKNOWN}" "$oph_fresh" "$phy_sev")"
    rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-ACOUSTIC	IMPULSE ($ss_kind, score $ss_score) ${ss_age}s ago (op-home=${oph_label:-missing} fresh=$oph_fresh)"$'\n'
    if [ "$phy_sev" != "$phy_sev_before" ]; then
      phy_msg="$phy_msg; ROOM EAR heard an impulse (${ss_kind}, score $ss_score) ${ss_age}s ago while operator AWAY (investigate)"
    else
      phy_msg="$phy_msg; acoustic impulse ${ss_age}s ago (op-home=${oph_label:-unknown} — routine/uncorroborated, not escalating)"
    fi
  else
    if [ -n "$ss_age" ]; then
      rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-ACOUSTIC	no impulse (ear live; newest scored window ${ss_age}s ago, score ${ss_score:-?} ${ss_kind:-rhythmic/grindable})"$'\n'
    else
      rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-ACOUSTIC	quiet (ear live, nothing scored in the window — the log is change-gated, so no line = no interesting sound)"$'\n'
    fi
  fi
else
  # say WHY it skipped — a not-me must read as skipped-for-this-reason, never vanish into a green pass
  rawfacts+="PHYSICAL-ACOUSTIC	UNKNOWN (soundscape ear stale/absent at $SCAPE_TICK) — not escalating, and its silence is NOT read as quiet (honest-fusion)"$'\n'
fi

# ---- FUSE: overall verdict = worst REACHABLE axis (rank()/fuse_posture defined up top, shared with --test)
fuse_posture "$out_sev" "$net_sev" "$phy_sev"

# cache the verdict for the META-fusion (mesh-situation reads this cheaply instead of re-running the
# heavy external axis). One line: <iso-ts>\t<out_sev>\t<net_sev>\t<phy_sev>\t<verdict>
printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$TS" "$out_sev" "$net_sev" "$phy_sev" "$verdict" > "$STATEFILE" 2>/dev/null || true

if [ "$RAW" -eq 1 ]; then printf '%s' "$rawfacts"; exit "$ec"; fi

# --edge: only emit when verdict changes (suppress identical repeats for cron wiring)
if [ "$EDGE" -eq 1 ] && [ "$verdict" = "$prev_verdict" ]; then exit "$ec"; fi

# --edge posts ONE terse verdict line (not the 4-line report — that's high-bulk board noise; mirrors
# mesh-stress's one-line edge). Full detail stays available via `mesh-perimeter` or ~/.mesh/.perimeter.state.
if [ "$EDGE" -eq 1 ]; then
  case "$verdict" in
    CALM|NOMINAL)   mark=perimeter-calm ;;
    ALERT|CRITICAL) mark=perimeter-alert ;;
    *)              mark=perimeter-notice ;;
  esac
  # surface the DRIVING axis's reason (the one whose severity == the fused verdict), trimmed to 1 line
  if   [ "$out_sev" = "$verdict" ]; then reason="$out_msg"
  elif [ "$net_sev" = "$verdict" ]; then reason="$net_msg"
  elif [ "$phy_sev" = "$verdict" ]; then reason="$phy_msg"
  else reason="$out_msg"; fi
  reason="$(printf '%s' "$reason" | cut -c1-90)"
  # DATE THE LINE (task:perimeter-log-has-no-timestamps-on-553-lines, genome 2026-08-21 -> health):
  # the cron wiring is `mesh-perimeter --edge >> ~/.mesh/perimeter.log` (reflexes.cron:84), i.e. a bare
  # append of stdout, so whatever this printf omits is omitted FOREVER -- 572 of 572 lines carried no
  # timestamp and a fired [perimeter-alert] was UNDATABLE: an alert from today read exactly like one
  # from July. mtime dates only the NEWEST line. The fix has to be here, at the emit, because nothing
  # downstream can recover a time the writer never wrote.
  # READER SWEPT BEFORE CHANGING THE SHAPE (a-format-fix-must-sweep-every-reader): the only consumer
  # of this log is mesh-sensor-tape's extract_token, whose FIRST step already strips `^<iso-ts>\s+\S+\s+`
  # -- so a leading ts followed by the bracket is eaten and the token still resolves. Verified against
  # the unmodified reader on all three shapes: undated -> CALM, dated notice -> CALM, dated alert ->
  # ALERT. mesh-awaydigest matches the mark with an unanchored TAG_RE and is indifferent to a prefix.
  # A ts prefix is therefore additive here; do NOT reorder it after the bracket (the reader's strip is
  # anchored at ^ and expects ts-then-token, and a redundant second strip would be a vacuous gate).
  edge_line "$TS" "$mark" "$HOST" "$out_sev" "$net_sev" "$phy_sev" "$reason"
  exit "$ec"
fi

printf '=== mesh-perimeter %s @ %s — %s ===\n' "$HOST" "$TS" "$verdict"
printf ' OUTSIDE  [%-11s] %s\n' "$out_sev" "$out_msg"
printf ' NETWORK  [%-11s] %s\n' "$net_sev" "$net_msg"
printf ' PHYSICAL [%-11s] %s\n' "$phy_sev" "$phy_msg"
exit "$ec"
